  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Write $2+\\frac{3-x}{1-x}$ as a single fraction.",
  "worked_solution": "Using the fact that $2=\\frac{2}{1},$ we can write the expression as\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{1}+\\frac{3-x}{1-x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. So, we put both fractions over a common denominator of $1-x$ by multiplying the top and bottom of the first fraction by $1-x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{1}+\\frac{3-x}{1-x} & = \\\\ \\frac{1-x}{1-x}\\cdot \\frac{2}{1}+\\frac{3-x}{1-x} & = \\\\ \\frac{2(1-x)}{1-x}+\\frac{3-x}{1-x} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the fractions and simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(1-x)}{1-x}+\\frac{3-x}{1-x} & = \\\\ \\frac{2(1-x)+(3-x)}{1-x} & = \\\\ \\frac{2-2x+3-x}{1-x} & = \\\\ \\frac{5-3x}{1-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
